7. JOB PRIORITY
Jobs shall support priority values.
Initial priority classes:
0  = LOW
10 = NORMAL
20 = HIGH
30 = CRITICAL
Higher-priority jobs execute before lower-priority jobs where worker capacity permits.
Priority shall never permit unauthorized bypass of project, authorization, constraint, or validation controls.

8. JOB DEPENDENCIES
Jobs may depend on other jobs.
system.job_dependencies
Fields:
job_id UUID NOT NULL
depends_on_job_id UUID NOT NULL
dependency_type VARCHAR(32) NOT NULL
created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL
Primary key:
(job_id, depends_on_job_id)
A dependent job cannot become executable until all required dependencies have reached an acceptable terminal state.

9. DEPENDENCY TYPES
Initial dependency types:
REQUIRED
OPTIONAL
A REQUIRED dependency must complete successfully.
An OPTIONAL dependency may fail without automatically preventing execution.

10. EXECUTION GRAPH
The Job & Worker Execution System supports directed execution graphs.
Example:
PROJECT REVISION
        ↓
MODEL VALIDATION
        ↓
GEOMETRY
   ↙    ↓    ↘
PLAN  SECTION  ELEVATION
   ↘    ↓    ↙
DRAWING COMPOSITION
        ↓
DOCUMENT PACKAGE
        ↓
HASH
        ↓
ACCEPTANCE
The Runtime Orchestrator creates the execution graph.
Workers execute individual nodes.

11. JOB CLAIMING
Workers shall claim jobs atomically.
Conceptually:
BEGIN

SELECT executable job
FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED

UPDATE job
SET status = CLAIMED

COMMIT
This prevents multiple workers from simultaneously claiming the same job.

12. WORKER REGISTRATION
Workers shall register with the execution subsystem.
system.workers
Fields:
id UUID PRIMARY KEY
worker_type VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL
