13. WORKER STATES
Workers use:
STARTING
IDLE
BUSY
DRAINING
OFFLINE
FAILED
A worker that stops sending heartbeats may be considered unavailable.

14. WORKER HEARTBEAT
Workers shall periodically publish:
worker_id
status
active_jobs
capacity
timestamp
version
The runtime system uses heartbeat state to determine worker availability.

15. WORKER CLAIM RECORD
Every job execution attempt shall be recorded.
system.job_attempts
Fields:
id UUID PRIMARY KEY
job_id UUID NOT NULL
worker_id UUID NOT NULL
attempt_number INTEGER NOT NULL
status VARCHAR(32) NOT NULL
started_at TIMESTAMPTZ
completed_at TIMESTAMPTZ
error JSONB
metrics JSONB
created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL
Unique:
UNIQUE(job_id, attempt_number)
This creates an execution history independent of the current job state.

16. HEARTBEAT TIMEOUT
A worker execution may be considered stale when:
last_heartbeat_at
exceeds the configured worker timeout.
The job is then eligible for recovery.
The system must distinguish:
worker failure
from:
job failure
A worker may fail while the underlying job remains recoverable.

17. JOB LEASE
A claimed job receives a lease.
Conceptually:
lease_until
is associated with the current attempt.
A worker must renew its lease while executing.
If the lease expires, another worker may reclaim the job after recovery safeguards are applied.

18. IDEMPOTENT EXECUTION
Every worker operation shall be designed to tolerate retry.
The job system must assume:
worker crash
network interruption
API timeout
process restart
machine failure
temporary dependency failure
may occur.
A retry must not silently create duplicate project mutations.

19. COMMAND IDEMPOTENCY
For model-changing operations, the execution key shall include:
project_id
command_id
source_revision_id
The Model Service verifies that the command has not already been committed.
This protects the Project Model from duplicate execution.

20. JOB RETRY POLICY
Default retry policy:
maximum_attempts = 3
Retryable conditions include:
temporary database failure
worker interruption
temporary network failure
temporary storage failure
service unavailable
resource exhaustion
Non-retryable conditions include:
invalid project state
authorization failure
constraint violation
invalid command
invalid model
invalid code manifest
corrupt source data

21. RETRY BACKOFF
Retries shall use exponential backoff.
Conceptually:
attempt 1 → short delay
attempt 2 → longer delay
attempt 3 → final retry
The exact delay remains configuration rather than domain logic.

22. DEAD-LETTER STATE
Jobs exceeding their retry policy enter:
FAILED
with a machine-readable failure record.
Future production deployments may additionally provide:
dead_letter
queue semantics.

23. JOB ERROR MODEL
Errors shall contain:
code
message
category
retryable
service
worker_id
job_id
details
timestamp
Example:
{
  "code": "GEOMETRY_GENERATION_FAILED",
  "category": "GEOMETRY",
  "retryable": true,
  "service": "geometry-worker",
  "details": {}
}

24. JOB PROGRESS
Workers shall report progress.
The minimum range is:
0–100
Progress must represent meaningful computational completion rather than arbitrary elapsed time.
Example:
0   = queued
10  = model loaded
30  = geometry generated
60  = geometry validated
80  = drawings regenerated
100 = complete

25. JOB EVENTS
Every significant state transition shall create an event.
system.job_events
Fields:
id UUID PRIMARY KEY
