failure analysis
cost analysis
worker scaling

42. RESOURCE LIMITS
Jobs may specify resource requirements.
Example:
cpu
memory
storage
gpu
timeout
Example:
{
  "cpu": 2,
  "memory_mb": 4096,
  "gpu": false,
  "timeout_seconds": 900
}
The scheduler uses these requirements to select an appropriate worker.

43. WORKER CAPABILITY MODEL
Workers may advertise capabilities.
Example:
geometry-worker:
    geometry
    spatial-validation

documentation-worker:
    drawing-generation
    sheet-composition

export-worker:
    pdf
    package-generation
The scheduler shall only assign jobs to workers capable of executing the requested job type.

44. WORKER VERSION COMPATIBILITY
Each worker reports:
worker_version
domain_version
Jobs may specify minimum compatible versions.
This prevents incompatible worker implementations from consuming jobs created against newer domain contracts.

45. ARTIFACT REFERENCES
Large job outputs shall not be embedded directly into PostgreSQL JSONB payloads.
Instead, the job result may contain:
artifact_id
storage_reference
content_hash
content_type
size
The object-storage layer becomes responsible for large binary artifacts.

46. JOB INPUT IMMUTABILITY
Once a job reaches:
CLAIMED
its computational input shall be immutable.
If the underlying project state changes, the existing job is either:
completed against its recorded source state
or:
invalidated
A running job shall never silently switch to a newer model version.

47. SOURCE STATE LOCK
Jobs that modify the Project Model shall identify:
expected_revision
expected_model_version
The domain service verifies those values before committing changes.
If the expected state is no longer current:
STALE_COMMAND
is returned.
The command is not applied against the newer state without explicit orchestration.

48. CONCURRENCY CONTROL
OneDraft shall use optimistic concurrency control for project model mutations.
The authoritative comparison is:
expected_revision
against:
current_revision
A mismatch prevents an unsafe mutation.

49. JOB TRANSACTION BOUNDARY
The job lifecycle itself and the domain transaction remain distinct.
Conceptually:
JOB CLAIM
 ↓
WORKER EXECUTION
 ↓
DOMAIN TRANSACTION
 ↓
DOMAIN RESULT
 ↓
JOB COMPLETION
A failed domain transaction shall not be represented as a successful job.

50. JOB COMPLETION
A job may transition to:
COMPLETED
only when:
required computation completed
required domain transaction committed
required validation completed
required result persisted
have all succeeded.

51. PARTIAL SUCCESS
Where a job contains multiple independent operations, the result may identify:
completed_items
failed_items
skipped_items
However, partial success shall never be interpreted as complete project success unless the job contract explicitly permits partial completion.

52. JOB OBSERVABILITY
The execution layer shall expose:
job status
worker status
queue depth
active jobs
failed jobs
retry count
execution duration
to administrative and operational interfaces.

