80. TIMEOUT POLICY
Jobs may specify execution timeouts.
Timeouts are divided into:
queue timeout
execution timeout
validation timeout
A timeout produces a structured failure state.
The job may be retried when the failure is classified as recoverable.

81. RESOURCE EXHAUSTION
Workers shall detect:
memory exhaustion
CPU exhaustion
storage exhaustion
GPU exhaustion
external service exhaustion
Resource exhaustion shall be recorded separately from logical domain failure.
This distinction allows infrastructure scaling rather than unnecessary model retries.

82. WORKER DRAINING
A worker may enter:
DRAINING
The worker stops accepting new jobs while completing active jobs.
This permits safe deployment and maintenance.

83. WORKER SHUTDOWN
Graceful shutdown sequence:
DRAIN
 ↓
STOP CLAIMING
 ↓
COMPLETE ACTIVE JOBS
 ↓
ACKNOWLEDGE JOBS
 ↓
OFFLINE
If graceful completion is impossible, leases allow another worker to recover the affected jobs.

84. JOB RECOVERY
Recovery process:
detect stale lease
 ↓
verify worker unavailable
 ↓
record worker failure
 ↓
release job
 ↓
increment attempt
 ↓
apply retry policy
 ↓
requeue
Recovery must preserve the original execution history.

85. DATABASE CONSISTENCY
Job state changes shall be transactional.
When a job completion causes a domain-state mutation, the application shall ensure that the domain result and corresponding job state cannot become misleadingly divergent.
Where distributed transaction semantics are impractical, the system shall use durable event records and reconciliation workers.

86. RECONCILIATION WORKER
The system shall include a:
system-reconciliation-worker
responsible for detecting inconsistencies such as:
job marked completed without result
artifact exists without completion record
worker lease expired
execution incomplete despite all jobs completed
dependent job blocked incorrectly
document package missing expected artifact
The reconciliation worker does not silently modify domain state without recording an audit event.

87. SCHEDULER
The scheduler is responsible for selecting executable work.
Conceptually:
QUEUED JOBS
 ↓
FILTER DEPENDENCIES
 ↓
FILTER RESOURCE REQUIREMENTS
 ↓
FILTER WORKER CAPABILITIES
 ↓
FILTER PRIORITY
 ↓
CLAIM JOB
The scheduler must never bypass dependency or authorization requirements.

88. BACKPRESSURE
When computational demand exceeds worker capacity, the queue shall absorb the workload.
Example:
100 drawing jobs
20 available workers
The system retains:
80 queued jobs
rather than overwhelming the application layer.

89. RATE LIMITING
The execution system may limit:
jobs per project
jobs per organization
jobs per user
jobs per worker
This prevents a single workload from monopolizing the execution system.
Limits shall be configurable.

90. FAIRNESS
Where multiple organizations share infrastructure, scheduling should prevent sustained starvation of lower-priority tenants.
The initial MVP may use priority plus FIFO ordering.
Future production scheduling may implement weighted fairness.

91. JOB RETENTION
Completed job records shall remain available for operational and audit purposes.
Retention policy may distinguish:
active jobs
completed jobs
failed jobs
audit events
execution metrics
Historical project execution records shall not be deleted merely because their associated job has aged beyond operational retention.

92. EXECUTION RECONSTRUCTION
Given:
execution_id
the system shall be able to reconstruct:
request
 ↓
root job
 ↓
child jobs
 ↓
worker attempts
 ↓
results
 ↓
project revision
 ↓
generated artifacts
This becomes part of the OneDraft provenance architecture.

93. FAILURE RECONSTRUCTION
Given a failed execution, the system shall be able to identify:
which job failed
which worker executed it
which attempt failed
what input state was used
what error occurred
whether the error was retryable
what downstream jobs were blocked

